# Configuration Tier 4 adds on top of the shared options in firmware/common.
#
# The first block is inherited from Tier 2, which taught the device to verify
# the service it talks to. Tier 4 keeps every word of it: the manifest is
# fetched over that same verified connection, and a signed manifest does not
# make an unverified connection acceptable.

menu "Tier 2 authenticated service"

config COURSE_OTA_SERVICE_NAME
	string "Name the service certificate must carry"
	default "ota.course.example"
	help
	  The device connects to CONFIG_COURSE_OTA_HOST, a literal address, and
	  then requires the certificate presented there to carry this name.

	  The name is never resolved. There is no DNS resolver in this image.
	  It is the value the TLS layer compares the certificate against, which
	  is why a wrong name is refused even though the address was right.

config COURSE_OTA_TLS_PORT
	int "OTA service TLS port"
	default 8443
	help
	  Tier 2 moves release records, firmware bytes, and status events to
	  this port. Health and the Course environment marker stay on the plain
	  HTTP port, because a safety check must not depend on the control it
	  is used to test.

config COURSE_TRUST_ANCHOR_FINGERPRINT
	string "Fingerprint of the trust anchor compiled into this image"
	default "unknown"
	help
	  Printed at boot so a stale trust anchor diagnoses itself instead of
	  looking like a broken network. ./course build firmware writes this
	  value, and ./course service certificate prints the same one.

	  The device does not compute it. Computing a digest on the board to
	  describe material the board already holds would prove nothing that the
	  build cannot state.

endmenu

menu "Tier 4 release policy"

config COURSE_SECURITY_COUNTER
	int "Security counter of the running image"
	default 1
	help
	  The anti-rollback counter this image was signed with. The same value
	  goes into the signed MCUboot image TLV and into the signed Release
	  manifest, and ./course build firmware writes it from the one constant
	  in internal/courseapp/tier04.go so the two cannot disagree.

	  The application refuses a manifest whose counter is lower than this
	  one. Equal is allowed; lower never is. That check saves a wasted
	  download and is not the boundary: the bootloader is what actually
	  stops a downgrade, by comparing the candidate's counter against the
	  counter in the image already in the primary slot.

	  It is deliberately not derived from the human-readable version.
	  "Human-readable versions override the security counter" is the
	  failure this tier is about.

config COURSE_HARDWARE_REVISION
	int "Product hardware revision this build asserts"
	default 1
	help
	  The product hardware revision a release is targeted at, compared
	  against the manifest's signed hardware_revision_min..max range.

	  It is asserted, not read. The chip reports its silicon stepping from
	  eFuse and the boot banner prints that beside this value, but nothing
	  on this part reports which product a board was built into. Product
	  identity is asserted and protected; a value the device reads is not a
	  claim anybody signed.

config COURSE_RELEASE_CHANNEL
	string "Release channel this device follows"
	default "stable"
	help
	  One of stable or candidate. The device refuses a manifest published
	  to any other channel, even a correctly signed one.

	  This is the check that has no attacker behind it. Nobody without the
	  Release signing key can produce a manifest that gets this far, so a
	  channel mismatch means whoever holds the key published something for
	  the wrong devices. The device cannot tell that from a leaked key, and
	  refusing is the same either way.

endmenu

source "Kconfig.zephyr"

config COURSE_SIGNING_KEY_FINGERPRINT
	string "Fingerprint of the image verification key this image was built beside"
	default "unknown"
	help
	  Printed at boot so a Learner can see which key the build trusted, and
	  so a key regenerated after flashing diagnoses itself instead of looking
	  like a broken image.

	  Read the wording in main.c before changing this. The application cannot
	  see what the bootloader actually holds. This value comes from the same
	  build that produced the bootloader, so it is a claim about the build and
	  not an observation of the device. Verifying the bootloader itself is
	  Advanced Tier A's subject.

	  From Tier 4 the same fingerprint also describes the release manifest
	  verification key compiled into the application, because section 7 fixes
	  that one key signs both. Unlike the bootloader's copy, that one this
	  application does hold, and release_policy.c reports when it is absent.
